    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate leaning heavily on **Mickey Moniak**, our primary offensive engine with a 75.75 efficiency score. He draws a high-upside matchup against Gusto, bolstered by a significant park factor (+14%) and a favorable platoon dynamic (+14%) that positions him for a productive afternoon. Complementing him is **Josh Naylor**, whose 65.22 score highlights his reliability against Walbert Ure\u00f1a. While the SP skill here is manageable, **Josh Naylor**\u2019s ability to drive runs from the #5 spot makes him an essential core play. Our middle-tier, including **Alex Freeland** (56.85) and **Kyle Karros** (56.78), remain viable starts primarily due to their exploitation of slower pitching deliveries, which creates high-probability opportunities for stolen bases\u2014a critical focus as we combat the league's scarcity in that category.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Cole Young**, who fell just short of the 45.0 threshold with a 44.17 score. Despite the SP matchup against Walbert Ure\u00f1a, **Cole Young** was squeezed out of the lineup due to a combination of park regression (-5%) and a suboptimal #8 spot in the order, which further depressed his projected production relative to **Travis Bazzana**. Meanwhile, we are maintaining a strict adherence to our efficiency floor; both **Zach McKinstry** and **Edwin Arroyo** were comfortably benched for failing to clear the 40.0 mark against their respective tough assignments. We remain cautious regarding **Dalton Rushing** and **Alex Freeland**, as their spots in the lineup are still pending; should their real-world batting positions drop significantly lower than projected, we risk a further erosion of their efficiency scores. Finally, we continue to monitor **Vinnie Pasquantino**, who remains on the IL and provides no value until his health status changes.",
